y></table><h2 style=3D"color:#000;font:700 24px/31px nyt-cheltenham,georgia=
,serif;margin:0 0 10px"><span>Has Iran lost control of the Strait of Hormuz=
=3F</span></h2><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;m=
argin:0 0 15px"><span>Before the war, some 19 million barrels of crude oil =
were exported from the Middle East every day. That number slowed to a trick=
le after Iran closed the Strait of Hormuz, the main route through which thi=
s oil traveled. The result was a crippling global energy crisis felt as far=
 away as the Philippines.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georg=
ia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>But last month, according to Kpl=
er, a firm that tracks oil tankers, the numbers climbed back up to an avera=
ge of 16 million barrels a day =E2=80=94 not back to prewar levels, but not=
 so far off either. Some 6 million barrels of that oil is coming through la=
nd pipelines and ports on the Red Sea or the Gulf of Oman that bypass the S=
trait of Hormuz. But 10 million are making it through the strait itself.</s=
pan></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0=
 0 15px"><span>How did this happen=3F</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17p=
x/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The story of the Ira=
n war has in many ways become the story of Iran closing the strait, and the=
 U.S. trying to reopen it =E2=80=94 and so the fact that oil is moving agai=
n feels like a big deal. And in some ways, it is. The U.S. has clearly redu=
ced Iran=E2=80=99s leverage over this critical waterway.</span></p><p style=
=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>B=
ut that doesn=E2=80=99t mean that the price of oil is going down any time s=
oon. And it doesn=E2=80=99t mean the U.S. is on the verge of winning the wa=
r either. It could even make Iran more dangerous.</span></p><p style=3D"fon=
t:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D=
"font-weight:700">Oil is flowing again</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17=
px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>I spoke to my colle=
ague Peter Eavis, who has been monitoring oil flows since the start of the =
war. He told me that Iran is still attacking ships in the strait </span><a =
href=3D"https://nl.nytimes.com/f/newsletter/WoR_siHDquWap6pcnSekbA~~/AAAAAR=
A~/St35NTTbPVwwEzvIh4hU682HDDBphIxSkwcvnJ1GVGYhDE6LoRR3Awe7le8HqZ9onb9E8eWe=
lqd6JO28lLEg_61XchnRVuSCwFUzvj86NP0-L8G4L5HCWFzuFIDTVySAs-8txtw-coKG4t4CB2f=
W4gv5FZYxQRT16pDgM8OiPekeDbwK_dHgmtW33z4B6r6AYbs8jKHnZOXCQMfri-0p3WmDW7dGgq=
PjP4Qs_bg7ryc1KsDAWQFP3xRYOdWKF5akENCsKHDevniDHpC26xp7QrnnW_8oiDmeTP_pe6FwZ=
FPnqzzceXQgK9MkC_s_C-C4Z1mMm7AUMGxerz9lKZyLYKODQpsH51HEksd3ndK4NQaImLVyyNEX=
CwayRATChEyGCW-T0VHSp2q0mebnIF4Qtw~~" class=3D"css-sdwaa1" style=3D"color:#=
286ed0;-webkit-text-decoration:underline;text-decoration:underline">practic=
ally every day</a><span>. But oil is making it out simply because more tank=
ers are now willing to take the risk of trying to cross. And a combination =
of two things helps explain why.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25p=
x georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Since the spring, the U.S=
=2E military has been offering air protection to tankers willing to take th=
eir chances through the strait. Initially, there were few takers.</span></p=
><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px=
"><span>But as the war has dragged on, countries like Saudi Arabia and the =
United Arab Emirates have become increasingly desperate to get their oil ou=
t. They=E2=80=99re now offering huge amounts of money =E2=80=94 ten times m=
ore than prewar fees in some cases, Peter said =E2=80=94 to tanker operator=
s. Enough that many are now are saying yes.</span></p><p style=3D"font:norm=
al 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The upshot is =
that oil is flowing again, but at a huge cost =E2=80=94 to the U.S. militar=
y, to oil-exporting Gulf nations and in human lives. About two dozen crew m=
embers have died in Iranian and American strikes in the past month, Peter s=
aid.</span></p><table border=3D"0" cellpadding=3D"0" cellspacing=3D"0" styl=
e=3D"width:100%"><tbody><tr><td style=3D"text-align:center"><a href=3D"http=
